.0,-5.125986263010418e-06,0.0,-0.0426611602306366,0.0,0.0,-6.353653589030728e-05,0.0,0.0,-0.003903033910319209,-4.768370445162873e-07,0.0,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,0.0,-7.986990567587782e-06,-9.536738616588991e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,47,90506,3,387,264,9334,6573,315,5567,13,400,47,3,374,47035,8630,400,49,3,323,1221,400,48,3,374,47035,8630,400,50,12947,576,3082,315,279,12942,7071,374,220,24,9334,14924,13,7379,279,46342,315,9334,400,47,90506,12947,508,6405,921,1057,74309,23,220,16,24,24,23,671,17,15,47,735,2141,7,16,36750,11,16,36750,317,1502,20912,47,73315,320,15,11,220,22,17,701,5052,317,1502,20912,48,73315,320,22,17,11,220,22,17,701,13387,317,1502,20912,50,73315,320,15,11,220,15,701,7856,317,1502,20912,49,73315,320,22,17,11,220,15,701,41074,317,7633,1188,15,11,15,29621,7,22,17,11,15,29621,7,22,17,11,22,17,29621,7,15,11,22,17,29621,21621,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $PQRS$ be a square piece of paper. $P$ is folded onto $R$ and then $Q$ is folded onto $S$. The area of the resulting figure is 9 square inches. Find the perimeter of square $PQRS$. [asy]\n/* AMC8 1998 #20P */\nsize(1inch,1inch);\nlabel(\"$P$\", (0, 72), SE);\nlabel(\"$Q$\", (72, 72), SW);\nlabel(\"$S$\", (0, 0), NE);\nlabel(\"$R$\", (72, 0), NW);\ndraw((0,0)--(72,0)--(72,72)--(0,72)--cycle);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d4aa3c786c12d75fb56ae446d6fe2a1b97c77b6fe8e045d0d156a0b0cc34b4d7"},"seconds":10.40031123161316,"seed":463037097677423142,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by understanding the effect of the folds on the square piece of paper. Let the side length of the square \\(PQRS\\) be \\(s\\).\n\n1. **First Fold: \\(P\\) is folded onto \\(R\\)**\n\n   When point \\(P\\) is folded onto \\(R\\), the crease will be the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(PR\\). This crease will pass through the midpoint of \\(PR\\) and will be a vertical line halfway between \\(P\\) and \\(R\\). The length of this crease is \\(s\\), and it will divide the square into two congruent right triangles and a rectangle in the middle.\n\n2. **Second Fold: \\(Q\\) is folded onto \\(S\\)**\n\n   Similarly, when point \\(Q\\) is folded onto \\(S\\), the crease will be the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(QS\\). This crease will pass through the midpoint of \\(QS\\) and will be a horizontal line halfway between \\(Q\\) and \\(S\\). The length of this crease is \\(s\\), and it will divide the remaining part of the square into two congruent right triangles and a smaller square in the middle.\n\nAfter both folds, the resulting figure is a smaller square with side length \\(\\frac{s}{2}\\). The area of this smaller square is given as 9 square inches. Therefore, we can write the equation for the area of the smaller square as:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2 = 9\n\\]\nSolving for \\(s\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{s^2}{4} = 9 \\implies s^2 = 36 \\implies s = 6\n\\]\nThe perimeter of the original square \\(PQRS\\) is:\n\\[\n4s = 4 \\times 6 = 24\n\\]\nThus, the perimeter of square \\(PQRS\\) is \\(\\boxed{24}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{24}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:487"}
